     8Bit(3L)              OS-9 (News)              8Bit(3L)     NNAAMMEE                  8Bit   -   A   small  Library  to  manage  language         dependent character with 8th bit support.       FFUUNNCCTTIIOONNSS                  void sseettuupp__ccoonnvvttaabbllee (doit, rev)          int doit;          int rev;          void ccoonnvv__cchhaarrsseett (buf, siz)          char *buf;          int siz;          char ccoonnvv__cchhaarr (c)          char c;      DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN                     This library    allows    software   to   handle         different   standards  of  international  character         sets.  As  a  suggestion  all  characters should be         transformed  to a unique standard (ISO 8859/1). The         MNews  system  converts the local written character         to  the  ISO  character  set and the Newsreader can         convert it back if desired.              To use  the  library  you  have  to  call  first         sseettuupp__ccoonnvvttaabbllee(())  with  two  arguments.  The first         tells  the  library if converting should take place         and  the  second  one  if  the converting should be         done  to  or  from  ISO (e.g. MNews uses to ISO, so         the   2nd   parameter   is  FALSE,  the  Newsreader         converts  back from ISO, so the parameter is TRUE.)         To    convert    a    block   of   characters   use         ccoonnvv__cchhaarrsseett(())  with  two arguments. The first is a         pointer  to  a buffer where the data is placed (and         will  be  stored back) and the second is the number         of  bytes to convert. To convert a single character         use  ccoonnvv__cchhaarr(())  with  the  source character as an         argument;    the   matching   character   will   be     Page 1                            Printed (22 Dec 1992)     8Bit(3L)              OS-9 (News)              8Bit(3L)         returned.              The library  determinates  the  type of the used         character  set  by  examnin the enviroment variable         CCHHAARRSSEETT   to   examine   then   a  file  where  the         converting  table  is stored. The file has the name         of  the  value  of  the  enviroment variable and is         located  in //hh00//UUSSRR//LLIIBB//CCHHAARRSSEETT or can be specified         with   the   entry   CCHHAARRSSEETT..LLIIBB   in  the  Sysinfo         database.       FFOORRMMAATT OOFF AA CCOONNVVEERRTTIINNGG TTAABBLLEE                 Each line  in this file is a converting rule for         one  character. A line with a leading hash (`#') is         treated as a comment. The format of a line is                      <local-char>     <iso-char>         where  one  can  use several versions of expressing         these   characters.   The  type  of  expression  is         determinated  using  the prefix. Following prefixes         are allowed:            '' the following character is the used literal.             11....99 The number is treated as a decimal number.             00xx the   following   number   is   treated  as  a             hexadecimal  number in the range of a byte (0 -             255).             00 the number is teated as an octal number.             %% the number is treated as a binary number.              For hexadecimal  there may also be a dollar sign         (`$')  instead  of the `0x' and for decimal numbers         there  may  be  a  hash  sign  (`#')  to mark them.         NOTE:  never  use the hash sign at the beginning of         a line; this line will be treated as a comment.       Page 2                            Printed (22 Dec 1992)     8Bit(3L)              OS-9 (News)              8Bit(3L)     EEXXAAMMPPLLEE OOFF AA FFIILLEE 